## Models Covered
- **Phi-3.5 Mini** (Microsoft, 3.8B) — Matches GPT-3.5 level on many tasks
- **Mistral 7B** (Mistral AI, 7B) — Outperforms Llama 2 13B on many benchmarks
- **Llama 3.2 1B & 3B** (Meta, 1B / 3B) — Matches Phi-3 level, better instruction following
- **Qwen 2.5 0.5B / 1.5B** (Alibaba, 0.5B / 1.5B) — Exceeds Mistral 7B on many benchmarks despite tiny size
- **Gemma 2B & 7B** (Google, 2B / 7B) — 2B outperforms Mistral 7B on some tasks
- **TinyLlama 1.1B** (Community (Zhang et al.), 1.1B) — Llama 2-like architecture, optimized for tiny
- **OLMo 1B & 7B** (AI2 (Allen Institute), 1B / 7B) — Open weights, fully open training process
- **MPT-3B** (MosaicML, 3B) — Outperforms Llama 2 7B on instruction-following

## Key Findings

### Best Overall: Phi-3.5 Mini (3.8B) - Balance of capability, speed, efficiency
### Best Tiny: Qwen 2.5 0.5B - Incredible quality for 0.5B parameters
### Best Edge: Llama 3.2 1B - Runs on phones, reasonable capability
### Best Coding: Phi-3.5 or Qwen 2.5 - Both excel at code generation

## Insights for RAM

- Efficiency scaling: Parameters no longer determine capability. 3.8B models now compete with 13B+.
 - Quantization: 4-bit or 8-bit quantization reduces VRAM by 75% with minimal loss.
 - Local deployment: Most models here can run on consumer hardware with optimizations.
 - Emerging leaders: Qwen and Phi leading the 'capability-per-parameter' race.
 - Open vs proprietary: Open models now rival proprietary on many benchmarks.
 - Training cost: Training tiny models is accessible to researchers and startups.
 - Use-case driven: Pick by task (coding, chat, reasoning) not just parameters.


